==Russian==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ru|orv|годъ||time, period}}, from {{inh|ru|sla-pro|*godъ}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ru}} {{m|ine-pro|*gʰedʰ-||to unite, be associated, be suitable}}. Cognates include Ukrainian {{l|uk|годi}} (enough), Bulgarian {{l|bg|годе}} ([[ever]], as in {{l|bg|кой-годе}} = whoever), Czech {{l|cs|hod}}, Polish {{l|pl|gódy}} (feast), Upper Sorbian [[hody]] (Christmas), Lower Sorbian [[gódy]] (Christmas), Latvian {{l|lv|gads}} (year), German {{l|de|Gatte}} (husband) and {{l|de|gätlich}} (convenient), as well as English [[gather]] and [[good]], Dutch {{l|nl|goed}}, and German {{l|de|gut}}. Descendants include Russian {{l|ru|пого́да}}, {{l|ru|вы́года}}, {{l|ru|уго́да}}, {{l|ru|го́дный}}, and {{l|ru|годи́ться}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ru-IPA}}
* {{audio|Ru-год.ogg|Audio|lang=ru}}

===Noun===
{{ru-noun+|e|or|c||(1)|or|c||(1)(2)|лет|par=+|loc=в +}}

# [[year]]
#: {{ux|ru|[[из го́да в год]]|year in and year out, from year to year|inline=y}}
#: {{ux|ru|[[в]] [[этот|э́том]] '''году́'''|this year|inline=y}}
#: {{ux|ru|[[года́ми]]|for years; for years on end|inline=y}}
#: {{ux|ru|[[девяностый|девяно́стые]] '''го́ды'''|the '90s|inline=y}}
#: {{ux|ru|[[учебный|уче́бный]] '''год'''|school year; academic year|inline=y}}
#: {{ux|ru|[[новый|Но́вый]] год|New Year’s Day|inline=y}}
#: {{ux|ru|[[он|Ему́]] [[два́дцать оди́н]] год.|He is 21 years old.|inline=y}}
#: {{ux|ru|[[он|Ему́]] [[два́дцать три]] го́да.|He is 23 years old.''|inline=y}}
#: {{ux|ru|[[он|Ему́]] [[два́дцать пять]] [[лето|лет]].|He is 25 years old.''|inline=y}}
#: {{ux|ru|[[ты́сяча]] [[девятьсо́т]] [[девяно́сто]] [[второ́й]] год|(year) 1992 (''in words'')|inline=y}}
#: {{ux|ru|[[две]] [[тысяча|ты́сячи]] [[оди́ннадцатый]] год|(year) 2011 (''in words'')|inline=y}}
#: {{ux|ru|[[с]] [[каждый|ка́ждым]] '''го́дом'''|from year to year, year by year, year after year|inline=y}}

====Usage notes====
* Use the appropriate case of '''год''' after simple or compound numbers ending in {{m|ru|оди́н}} (1), {{m|ru|два}} (2), {{m|ru|три}} (3), or {{m|ru|четы́ре}} (4), except for 11, 12, 13 and 14 (and 111 - 114, 211 - 214 etc.) - for these and all other numbers, and with [[adverb]]s of quantity (''{{m|ru|мно́го}}'', ''{{m|ru|ско́лько}}'', etc.), use the appropriate case of {{m|ru|ле́то}}.
* After numbers that must be followed by a nominative or genitive '''singular''', the appropriate form of '''[[год]]''' is used. After numbers that must be followed by a '''genitive plural''', the form '''[[лет]]''' is used. When the number is in the dative, instrumental, or prepositional case, the appropriate form of '''[[год]]''' must be used. Examples: '''оди́н год''' (nominative singular), '''два го́да''' (genitive singular), '''три го́да''' (genitive singular), '''пять лет''' (genitive plural); but '''бо́льше трёх лет''' (genitive plural), '''бо́льше пяти́ лет''' (genitive plural); and '''пяти́ года́м''' (dative plural), '''пятью́ года́ми''' (instrumental plural), '''o пяти́ года́х''' (prepositional plural).

